          "data_flow": [],
          "evidence": [
            {
              "file": "pypdf/_cmap.py",
              "lines": "239-303",
              "snippet": "",
              "why": "All calls to _check_token_length (and the MAX_CMAP_CODE_BYTES_LIMIT/MAX_CMAP_STRING_BYTES_LIMIT constants that bounded them) were deleted from parse_bfrange, so the hex tokens lst[0]/lst[1] (code range bounds), sq (bfrange list strings), and lst[2] (single destination) are no longer size-checked before int()/unhexlify(). A crafted /ToUnicode CMap with an oversized lst[0]/lst[1] inflates map_dict[-1] (via nbi/ceil), producing a huge zero-padded fmt string that is re-formatted and unhexlify'd on every iteration of the bounded-count-but-not-bounded-size while-loop, and an oversized sq token is unhexlify'd/decoded directly with no size cap; _check_mapping_size only limits the number of dict entries, not per-token length, so a single malicious PDF can drive excessive memory/CPU consumption during text extraction."
            }
          ],
